# Every claim filed as a single value, beside who it says owns the edge. One row per such claim,
# `violates` where the answer is that the bound is where the measurements fell.
#
# The schema's two sentences, side by side
#   The rule puts two of the schema's own sentences beside each other. `boundOrigin` with
#   `absent/reason = none` is documented as: nothing sets it, the bound is where the measurements
#   fell, not where a rule put them, and there is no lever to look for. A single value has no
#   range, so nothing fell anywhere. The answer describes a spread the claim does not have, and it
#   is the answer a receiver would act on (measure again and the bound will move), against a
#   figure filed as though measuring again would change nothing.
#
# The schema already names the right answer
#   It is in the entry directly below. `notApplicable` is "the quantity has no author to ask
#   about: an observed count, a derived residual". An exact observation is that. `derived` is the
#   other honest answer, and most single-value claims here give it: the figure is stated in a
#   sibling element (`Nameplate/amountOrigin`, `LumpyQuantum/origin`, and on an elimination
#   `asrt:against` plus `asrt:between`), which is what allows filing it without a width. So this
#   rule does not forbid an exact claim. It asks that the exactness have an author, and the list
#   has a member for every honest one.
#
# The third of three crossings
#   Two rules already cross a claim's width with `narrowsWhen`: a single value must file
#   `notApplicable`, a range must not. This crosses the same width with `boundOrigin`, the other
#   half of the required pair. Neither column alone shows it: `none` is a common `boundOrigin`
#   answer and unremarkable on its own, and so is the width.
#
# What would refute it
#   A quantity that is truly exact, truly unowned, and truly not an observed count. The schema's
#   own description of `none` says no such thing exists, since an unowned bound is one that
#   measurement placed.
-- pm:Claim/pm:boundOrigin with pm:absent/reason = none on a claim where low = high.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT c.filing, c.layer,
           c.origin_absent IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'none' AS violates,
           format('%s claim %s: %s %s exactly, and nothing is said to set it',
                  c.owns, c.seq, c.mode, c.unit) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(epistemics/point_claims.sqlc)
    ) c
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'bound_fell_with_no_range'
